1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:05,509
In Part One, we separated a written claim, its proof, and the
meaning of its symbols.

2
00:00:06,032 --> 00:00:09,632
Now we return to a calculation we can actually inspect.

3
00:00:10,155 --> 00:00:16,962
One third is an exact number… but a decimal display has room for
only finitely many digits.

4
00:00:17,485 --> 00:00:22,705
If we stop at zero point three three three… we have changed the
number.

5
00:00:23,227 --> 00:00:30,241
Multiply that displayed decimal by three, and we get zero point nine
nine nine… rather than one.

6
00:00:30,764 --> 00:00:34,764
The missing amount is small, but a proof cannot quietly discard it.

7
00:00:35,286 --> 00:00:38,208
We can handle that problem in two different ways.

8
00:00:38,730 --> 00:00:45,647
Keep fractions exact… and when a value is not known exactly, keep a
justified interval around it.

9
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:51,952
This is Part Two of Landau Windows ACL Two: exact arithmetic with
imperfect information.

10
00:00:52,474 --> 00:00:56,911
Our aim is to make every later numerical claim carry its own
guarantee.

11
00:00:59,033 --> 00:01:02,912
A fraction gives a number a finite description using integers.

12
00:01:03,435 --> 00:01:08,909
For two thirds, record a numerator of two… and a denominator of
three.

13
00:01:09,432 --> 00:01:13,032
The denominator must be positive in the convention we will use.

14
00:01:13,555 --> 00:01:18,815
Four sixths describes the same rational number, so different pairs
can have the same meaning.

15
00:01:19,338 --> 00:01:25,798
ACL2 reduces rational numbers to a canonical form; four sixths is
read as two thirds.

16
00:01:26,321 --> 00:01:32,515
Negative fractions also have finite descriptions: record a sign
together with the numerator and denominator.

17
00:01:33,038 --> 00:01:37,123
This does not add a new kind of infinite object to Peano Arithmetic.

18
00:01:37,645 --> 00:01:44,542
Signed integers and fractions can be encoded using natural numbers,
with arithmetic rules for their representations.

19
00:01:45,065 --> 00:01:52,915
For example, comparing two thirds with three quarters becomes
comparing two times four… with three times three.

20
00:01:53,438 --> 00:01:57,656
Eight is less than nine, so two thirds is less than three quarters.

21
00:01:58,179 --> 00:02:05,076
That is the sort of explicit arithmetic connection we need when we
move between a convenient notation and a weaker foundation.

22
00:02:05,598 --> 00:02:12,265
These examples illustrate that connection; they are not a full
formal interpretation of all of ACL2.

23
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:17,309
Let us add one third and one sixth, slowly.

24
00:02:17,832 --> 00:02:23,197
Split each third into two sixths… so one third becomes two sixths.

25
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:25,780
Now the denominators agree.

26
00:02:26,303 --> 00:02:31,438
Two sixths plus one sixth gives three sixths… which is one half.

27
00:02:31,961 --> 00:02:35,245
No decimal approximation was needed at any step.

28
00:02:35,768 --> 00:02:40,326
In ACL2, the expression on screen asks for exactly that sum.

29
00:02:40,848 --> 00:02:45,733
The slash inside a rational literal is part of its written number
representation.

30
00:02:46,256 --> 00:02:50,923
A slash at the start of a parenthesized expression is the division
operation.

31
00:02:51,445 --> 00:02:55,300
Both expressions here produce the same exact rational answer.

32
00:02:55,823 --> 00:03:00,526
The small theorem below records this equality in our separately
checked teaching book.

33
00:03:01,048 --> 00:03:08,321
Exact arithmetic solves rounding in these operations, but it does
not tell us every value we will later want to study.

34
00:03:08,844 --> 00:03:10,589
For those, we need bounds.

35
00:03:12,733 --> 00:03:16,673
Suppose we know that x lies between one half and three quarters.

36
00:03:17,195 --> 00:03:22,003
We draw the whole interval… and then place x somewhere inside it.

37
00:03:22,526 --> 00:03:28,550
Two thirds is one possible example, but the guarantee should not
depend on that particular choice.

38
00:03:29,073 --> 00:03:35,214
Move the point and the claim remains true… as long as it stays
between the endpoints.

39
00:03:35,736 --> 00:03:38,888
The square brackets mean that both endpoints are allowed.

40
00:03:39,411 --> 00:03:42,695
An interval is not a guess that its midpoint is correct.

41
00:03:43,218 --> 00:03:49,262
It is a claim that the value is at least the lower endpoint… and at
most the upper endpoint.

42
00:03:49,785 --> 00:03:54,452
Making that claim requires evidence; drawing the picture does not
supply it.

43
00:03:54,974 --> 00:04:00,126
We will first assume input bounds are justified and prove that our
arithmetic preserves them.

44
00:04:00,648 --> 00:04:06,139
Later episodes explain how the project constructs bounds for its
less elementary functions.

45
00:04:06,662 --> 00:04:11,402
Here the endpoints and the values in the ACL2 statements are
rational numbers.

46
00:04:13,533 --> 00:04:17,752
We need an explicit representation before we name an interval
function.

47
00:04:18,274 --> 00:04:21,074
Store the lower and upper endpoints in a pair.

48
00:04:21,597 --> 00:04:29,503
Cons builds that pair… car retrieves its first component… and cdr
retrieves its second component.

49
00:04:30,026 --> 00:04:33,480
The dot in this printed pair separates those two components.

50
00:04:34,003 --> 00:04:38,427
It is a dotted pair, rather than the proper list of digits used in
Part One.

51
00:04:38,950 --> 00:04:47,508
The function lw-intervalp checks that a pair exists, that both
endpoints are rational, and that the lower one does not exceed the
upper one.

52
00:04:48,030 --> 00:04:54,321
Here is the complete definition from the project's interval kernel,
copied into our isolated teaching book.

53
00:04:54,844 --> 00:05:03,029
Consp checks for a pair… rationalp checks each endpoint… and the
last comparison checks their order.

54
00:05:03,552 --> 00:05:08,048
Once we have read those checks, we can fold the definition into a
compact name.

55
00:05:08,571 --> 00:05:11,662
Now define the claim that the pair contains a value.

56
00:05:12,185 --> 00:05:18,718
Lw-contains requires a valid interval, a rational value, and the two
endpoint inequalities.

57
00:05:19,241 --> 00:05:22,780
The conjunction and means that all those requirements must hold.

58
00:05:23,303 --> 00:05:29,545
This is a test of a supplied interval and value; it does not
discover an unknown value for us.

59
00:05:30,068 --> 00:05:33,195
We will use its name as shorthand for that full claim.

60
00:05:35,333 --> 00:05:38,630
Take x in the interval from one half to three quarters.

61
00:05:39,153 --> 00:05:42,256
Take y in the interval from one quarter to one third.

62
00:05:42,779 --> 00:05:46,718
We want bounds for x plus y that work for every allowed choice.

63
00:05:47,241 --> 00:05:51,229
The smallest possible sum comes from adding the two lower endpoints.

64
00:05:51,752 --> 00:05:54,624
One half plus one quarter is three quarters.

65
00:05:55,147 --> 00:05:59,050
The largest possible sum comes from adding the two upper endpoints.

66
00:05:59,573 --> 00:06:02,664
Three quarters plus one third is thirteen twelfths.

67
00:06:03,186 --> 00:06:10,689
Here is lw-interval-add: cons stores the two sums, using car for
lower endpoints and cdr for upper endpoints.

68
00:06:11,212 --> 00:06:14,667
There is no search for the hidden values in this definition.

69
00:06:15,189 --> 00:06:18,414
The reason it works is that inequalities can be added.

70
00:06:18,936 --> 00:06:27,502
If each lower endpoint is below its value, their sum is below the
sum of the values… and the upper endpoints give the other
inequality.

71
00:06:28,024 --> 00:06:36,691
The theorem lw-contains-add says exactly that the output interval
contains x plus y whenever the two input intervals contain x and y.

72
00:06:37,214 --> 00:06:43,383
The letters in the source are left-value and right-value; those play
the roles of x and y in our picture.

73
00:06:43,906 --> 00:06:51,695
That is a general statement about all rational inputs satisfying the
hypotheses… not just the points we animated.

74
00:06:53,833 --> 00:06:55,821
Subtraction needs more care.

75
00:06:56,344 --> 00:07:00,635
Use the same two input intervals, but now consider x minus y.

76
00:07:01,158 --> 00:07:04,333
Subtracting the two lower endpoints would give one quarter.

77
00:07:04,856 --> 00:07:11,870
That cannot be a safe lower bound: choose x equal to one half… and y
equal to one third.

78
00:07:12,392 --> 00:07:15,895
Their difference is one sixth, which is smaller than one quarter.

79
00:07:16,418 --> 00:07:22,438
To make x minus y as small as possible, make x small… and y large.

80
00:07:22,961 --> 00:07:27,700
So the lower output is the lower endpoint of x minus the upper
endpoint of y.

81
00:07:28,223 --> 00:07:32,861
To make the difference large, make x large… and y small.

82
00:07:33,383 --> 00:07:37,844
The upper output is the upper endpoint of x minus the lower endpoint
of y.

83
00:07:38,367 --> 00:07:41,906
Those choices give the interval from one sixth to one half.

84
00:07:42,429 --> 00:07:48,065
The complete definition of lw-interval-sub shows the crossed
components explicitly.

85
00:07:48,588 --> 00:07:54,855
After folding it into its name, the theorem guarantees containment
for every allowed rational x and y.

86
00:07:55,377 --> 00:08:01,183
This crossing of endpoints is part of the mathematical guarantee,
not a cosmetic choice in the code.

87
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:07,070
Next multiply an interval by a fixed rational coefficient.

88
00:08:07,592 --> 00:08:12,320
For a coefficient of two, every point moves to twice its original
value.

89
00:08:12,842 --> 00:08:17,788
The interval from one half to three quarters becomes the interval
from one to three halves.

90
00:08:18,311 --> 00:08:21,462
A negative coefficient introduces one more step.

91
00:08:21,985 --> 00:08:29,350
Multiplication by minus two reflects the points across zero… as well
as doubling their distance from zero.

92
00:08:29,873 --> 00:08:33,691
The old lower endpoint now lands on the right, at minus one.

93
00:08:34,214 --> 00:08:38,408
The old upper endpoint lands on the left, at minus three halves.

94
00:08:38,930 --> 00:08:42,967
We must therefore reverse the endpoint order to store a valid
interval.

95
00:08:43,489 --> 00:08:47,077
Here is the full definition of lw-interval-scale.

96
00:08:47,600 --> 00:08:51,624
The if expression chooses a branch according to the sign of the
coefficient.

97
00:08:52,147 --> 00:08:59,912
A nonnegative coefficient keeps the order… and a negative
coefficient swaps which endpoint supplies the lower output.

98
00:09:00,435 --> 00:09:05,598
Zero belongs to the first branch and collapses the interval to the
single point zero.

99
00:09:06,121 --> 00:09:11,418
The corresponding containment theorem includes the hypothesis that
the coefficient is rational.

100
00:09:11,941 --> 00:09:17,686
We have now justified addition, subtraction, and multiplication by a
fixed rational number.

101
00:09:19,833 --> 00:09:23,070
How much uncertainty have we carried through the calculation?

102
00:09:23,592 --> 00:09:27,677
The width of an interval is its upper endpoint minus its lower
endpoint.

103
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:33,711
Our first interval has width one quarter… and our second has width
one twelfth.

104
00:09:34,233 --> 00:09:38,148
Their sum interval has width one third, the sum of those two widths.

105
00:09:38,671 --> 00:09:43,011
Subtraction also adds the input widths, because its endpoints cross.

106
00:09:43,533 --> 00:09:49,206
Multiplication by a fixed coefficient multiplies width by that
coefficient's absolute value.

107
00:09:49,729 --> 00:09:52,989
We should distinguish width from a symmetric error radius.

108
00:09:53,512 --> 00:09:59,415
For an interval centered on an approximation, the radius is the
distance from the center to either endpoint.

109
00:09:59,938 --> 00:10:03,368
A symmetric interval's full width is twice that radius.

110
00:10:03,891 --> 00:10:11,498
So a width of two hundredths means an error radius of one hundredth…
when the displayed approximation is its midpoint.

111
00:10:12,021 --> 00:10:17,403
The containment guarantee comes from the endpoints, whether or not
we choose to display a midpoint.

112
00:10:17,926 --> 00:10:22,774
Keeping these two quantities separate prevents a factor-of-two
mistake in an error budget.

113
00:10:24,900 --> 00:10:28,088
Consider the expression two times x minus y.

114
00:10:28,611 --> 00:10:33,302
Suppose x is enclosed from ninety-nine hundredths to one hundred and
one hundredths.

115
00:10:33,824 --> 00:10:39,206
Suppose y is enclosed from one hundred and ninety-nine hundredths to
two hundred and one hundredths.

116
00:10:39,729 --> 00:10:45,002
Both input intervals have an error radius of one hundredth around
their displayed centers.

117
00:10:45,524 --> 00:10:51,677
Doubling x doubles its error radius… and subtracting y adds the
other radius.

118
00:10:52,200 --> 00:10:55,400
The total output radius is therefore three hundredths.

119
00:10:55,923 --> 00:11:00,529
The resulting interval runs from minus three hundredths to plus
three hundredths.

120
00:11:01,052 --> 00:11:06,506
That interval crosses zero, so these bounds alone cannot establish a
positive result.

121
00:11:07,029 --> 00:11:11,853
Now suppose a more precise computation justifies narrower input
intervals.

122
00:11:12,376 --> 00:11:19,341
Enclose x from exactly one point zero zero one… to exactly one point
zero zero three.

123
00:11:19,864 --> 00:11:26,344
Enclose y from exactly one point nine nine nine… to exactly two
point zero zero one.

124
00:11:26,867 --> 00:11:33,364
These terminating decimals are exact fractions, and the narrower
bounds still require a new justification.

125
00:11:33,886 --> 00:11:37,389
The output now runs from one thousandth to seven thousandths.

126
00:11:37,912 --> 00:11:45,605
Its midpoint is four thousandths and its error radius is three
thousandths… so the entire interval is positive.

127
00:11:46,127 --> 00:11:51,133
Every endpoint in this example is calculated by the functions we
have already defined.

128
00:11:51,656 --> 00:11:56,783
An error budget tells us how accurate the ingredients must be for
the final claim we want.

129
00:11:58,900 --> 00:12:01,167
Let us isolate that final claim.

130
00:12:01,689 --> 00:12:07,229
If an interval crosses zero, its midpoint might be positive while an
allowed value is negative.

131
00:12:07,752 --> 00:12:11,958
If its lower endpoint equals zero, the interval still permits zero.

132
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:16,577
A strictly positive lower endpoint rules out both possibilities.

133
00:12:17,100 --> 00:12:21,282
Once x is at least that endpoint, x must be positive as well.

134
00:12:21,805 --> 00:12:26,386
The ACL2 comparison on screen checks only the stored lower endpoint.

135
00:12:26,909 --> 00:12:32,024
The theorem adds the essential hypothesis: the interval really
contains the value.

136
00:12:32,547 --> 00:12:38,402
Without that hypothesis, a positive pair of endpoints says nothing
about an unrelated quantity.

137
00:12:38,924 --> 00:12:44,742
This small implication turns a computed bound into a certificate of
a mathematical inequality.

138
00:12:45,265 --> 00:12:51,205
The same idea works above any rational threshold, by comparing the
lower endpoint with that threshold.

139
00:12:51,727 --> 00:12:56,624
The difficulty in a larger proof is to justify the enclosure and
make it narrow enough.

140
00:12:58,767 --> 00:13:02,573
There is a useful converse guarantee behind that last difficulty.

141
00:13:03,095 --> 00:13:07,447
Suppose the true value is above a threshold by more than an error
budget.

142
00:13:07,970 --> 00:13:12,721
Also suppose our interval contains that value and has width at most
that budget.

143
00:13:13,244 --> 00:13:16,783
Then its lower endpoint must already lie above the threshold.

144
00:13:17,306 --> 00:13:25,152
Here is a numerical picture: threshold zero… width budget one tenth…
and value one fifth.

145
00:13:25,674 --> 00:13:30,850
Any containing interval of width at most one tenth cannot stretch
all the way down to zero.

146
00:13:31,373 --> 00:13:35,167
To see the general reason, call the endpoints lower and upper.

147
00:13:35,689 --> 00:13:43,442
The value is no larger than upper… so the distance from lower to the
value is no greater than the full interval width.

148
00:13:43,965 --> 00:13:46,450
That distance is at most the error budget.

149
00:13:46,973 --> 00:13:50,718
Rearrange: lower is at least the value minus the budget.

150
00:13:51,241 --> 00:13:55,847
And the assumed strict margin makes that last quantity greater than
the threshold.

151
00:13:56,370 --> 00:14:03,255
The theorem lw-lower-gt-of-margin states this guarantee for rational
values, thresholds, and budgets.

152
00:14:03,777 --> 00:14:07,571
We are not asking the program to look up an unknown true value.

153
00:14:08,094 --> 00:14:15,985
The theorem says that when mathematics supplies a sufficient margin,
any justified interval with the required precision must reveal it.

154
00:14:16,508 --> 00:14:21,017
This is what will connect an error budget to a search that is
guaranteed to succeed.

155
00:14:23,133 --> 00:14:25,642
One boundary still deserves attention.

156
00:14:26,165 --> 00:14:30,189
The containment function we showed quantifies over rational values.

157
00:14:30,712 --> 00:14:36,409
It does not quietly turn an irrational number into an ordinary ACL2
rational object.

158
00:14:36,932 --> 00:14:41,950
Yet rational calculations can provide evidence for a bound on an
irrational quantity.

159
00:14:42,473 --> 00:14:49,426
For example, seven fifths squared is less than two… and three halves
squared is greater than two.

160
00:14:49,948 --> 00:14:58,441
Clear the positive denominators and these become the integer
comparisons forty-nine is less than fifty… and eight is less than
nine.

161
00:14:58,964 --> 00:15:02,382
Our teaching book checks those exact arithmetic facts.

162
00:15:02,905 --> 00:15:13,668
To conclude that the positive square root of two lies between seven
fifths and three halves, we also use the mathematical meaning of a
positive square root and the order of positive squares.

163
00:15:14,191 --> 00:15:20,179
The integer certificate and its interpretation are related, but they
are distinct proof obligations.

164
00:15:20,702 --> 00:15:30,047
The project follows that discipline: arithmetic constructions in
ACL2, and explicit interpretation statements connecting them to the
classical quantities.

165
00:15:30,570 --> 00:15:38,667
Peano Arithmetic can describe finite integer calculations; a
complete reduction must also explain what its encoded statements
mean.

166
00:15:39,189 --> 00:15:43,723
We should track that bridge as carefully as the numerical operations
themselves.

167
00:15:45,867 --> 00:15:49,939
We began with a decimal display that silently changed one third.

168
00:15:50,462 --> 00:15:54,123
Exact rational arithmetic removed that particular error.

169
00:15:54,645 --> 00:16:00,221
Intervals then let us carry justified uncertainty through addition,
subtraction, and scaling.

170
00:16:00,744 --> 00:16:08,295
We kept width separate from radius, composed an error budget, and
proved why a positive lower endpoint certifies positivity.

171
00:16:08,818 --> 00:16:14,200
A strict mathematical margin tells us when enough precision must
make that certificate appear.

172
00:16:14,723 --> 00:16:20,832
These are small reusable guarantees, and the interval definitions
come directly from this repository.

173
00:16:21,355 --> 00:16:28,445
Next we will lay down a finite grid, examine its intervals, and
return an address where a certified inequality holds.

174
00:16:28,968 --> 00:16:32,120
Checking a returned address is only one obligation.

175
00:16:32,642 --> 00:16:37,636
We must also prove that the search finds an address under the
intended hypotheses.

176
00:16:38,159 --> 00:16:42,959
That is where the exact bounds from this episode become part of the
Landau construction.
